WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.490
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Grant.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.110
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh hey, Martin.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.780
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, I've been
using the gcloud command line

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.050
tool from the Cloud
SDK a lot recently,

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.380
and I'd like to get a bit
more productive with it.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.960
Do you have any tips or tricks?

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.410
GRANT TIMMERMAN: I sure do.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.670
I have six tricks up
my sleeve to help you

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.425
with your productivity
when using gcloud.

00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:24.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.140
Tell me more.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.598
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.230
As you know, I'm trying to
learn the gcloud command line

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.620
tool a bit more.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.190
And there are new flags
that I'm unfamiliar with,

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.130
like all the new
features of Cloud Run.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.980
Is there a way I can quickly
learn these new features

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.568
and flags?

00:00:49.568 --> 00:00:50.860
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yes, there is.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.440
So here's a fun one.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.310
gcloud has an interactive mode
where you can see documentation

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.930
as you type.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:04.629
To start interactive mode,
run gcloud beta interactive.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:07.570
This will start a session
in your terminal providing

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:12.050
autocompletion and inline
documentation as you type.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:15.190
So for example, Cloud Run
has a lot of feature flags

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:16.780
you can specify.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:22.180
If you type gcloud
run deploy --,

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.218
you can go and toggle
through all the flags.

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:26.260
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, cool.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.120
Yeah, that's --max-instances.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.620
That's the one I
was looking for.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:30.120
Cool.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.210
All right.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.270
Great.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.180
Thank you for that tip.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.510
For number two, here's
a different issue.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:43.110
When I deploy to Cloud
Functions or to Cloud Run,

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.870
I keep getting the
same prompts in gcloud

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.260
when running those commands.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.640
So like it asks me for my
location for my Cloud Run

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.130
service, or it
asks me to specify

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:57.510
I want to deploy to Cloud
Run Managed or to Kubernetes.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.840
So I have to write these large
scripts with many command line

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.050
flags for this.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.700
How do I avoid that?

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.530
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, do you
know about gcloud configs?

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.500
MARTIN OMANDER: No, I don't.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.050
GRANT TIMMERMAN:
Oh, let me show you.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.650
So gcloud configs allow you
to save configuration options

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.060
so you don't need to
get prompted for them

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.280
and you don't need
to repeat them.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.680
So the general syntax is
gcloud config set, and then

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.560
your property and value.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.460
So for example, when
deploying to Cloud Run,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:39.420
you could run the command
of gcloud run deploy myapp,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.050
and then you provide
your --image.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:47.020
But then you might notice,
oh, you get prompted, OK,

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.880
do you want Cloud
Run fully managed

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.920
and which region do you want?

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.150
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.280
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Right.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.080
So let's go ahead
and cancel that.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.200
Or you could go and
set your config.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.660
So you can set your config
with gcloud config set

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:10.580
run/platform managed,
then maybe your run/region

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:14.140
to europe-west3.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.040
So if I do that, then
it'll update the properties

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.000
and I can simply rerun
the previous command.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.880
And you'll notice now,
I'm not prompted--

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.270
automatically just working.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.950
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, nice.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.340
I like fewer prompts.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.840
OK, cool.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.890
For number three, sometimes, I
need to include the project ID

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.000
with a command.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.100
I also switch between
projects often.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.470
And sometimes, I use
the wrong project.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.390
And I have actually,
in the past,

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.420
sometimes deployed code
to the wrong project.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.730
My manager did not like that.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:57.280
Is there an easier way
to manage the projects?

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.230
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yeah, there is.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.970
So I've faced this, too.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.300
It's a little annoying
specifying your project name

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.610
everywhere.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:10.015
And I don't really like
to mix up deploying

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:11.140
between different projects.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.745
That's sort of scary.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.360
But with gcloud, you can get
and set your current project.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.870
So in your scripts,
you don't need

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.320
to hardcode your project ID.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.370
So in the command line,
you can set the project

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:36.000
with gcloud config set project
and then your project name.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.560
Once you have done
that, then that

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.730
will be your default project
with all the gcloud commands.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.000
And you can also get
your current project

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.950
and store it in an
environment variable.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.210
How do you do that?

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.730
Well, you can go and type--

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.160
you can go and type--

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.900
let's say you want your project.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:05.260
Here's a little script
to set your project

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.360
inside this
environment variable.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.640
Then you can use this
environment variable anywhere,

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.570
like when using gcloud.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:13.950
So here's a little example.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:20.200
When using gcloud build submit,
you can provide your tag

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.320
and you always
need your project.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:27.330
And so it'll just
recognize that and work.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.850
So I use this all the
time in my scripts

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.860
when deploying to Cloud Run.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:33.630
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, nice.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:38.220
No more deploying code to
the wrong project for me.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.470
So for number four,
I switch around

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:41.880
between a few
different projects,

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.900
and I sometimes forget which
projects have billing enabled.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.850
Many Cloud Services
require billing

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:49.660
to be enabled before using them.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.650
So how can I check that, Grant?

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.990
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, that's
a good question, Martin.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:58.500
So with gcloud, there's
a relatively new command

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.270
you can use to go
and check this.

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:07.515
You can run the following
gcloud beta billing command.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.150
So after you run this,
you get your project.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.430
You describe it.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:22.400
And you'll get a Boolean
value of True or False.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.610
And so this will
indicate if billing's

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:28.100
enabled for that project.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.500
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, cool.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.970
Now I can check if billing is
enabled for all my projects

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.210
without having to go to the web
browser and the Cloud Console

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:36.610
over there.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:37.810
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yep.

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:38.893
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:06:38.893 --> 00:06:42.520
So for number five, when I've
deployed a private Cloud Run

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:46.120
service, I can't really
test it using curl.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.530
If I try to curl it, I get the
response, error, forbidden.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:53.695
How do I fix that?

00:06:53.695 --> 00:06:54.570
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yep.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:56.880
So a Cloud Run service
that wasn't deployed

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.540
with --allow-unathenticated
is not

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.900
accessible with
unauthorized requests.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.240
So for example,
we can go and try

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:11.241
curling a Cloud Run service,
and we'll see an error.

00:07:11.241 --> 00:07:15.320
For example, like 403 forbidden.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:19.940
Not anybody on the public web
can test our proprietary Cloud

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.330
Run service.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.990
MARTIN OMANDER: That's
what I'm getting, yep.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:25.520
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Yep.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:29.030
But that's really very
difficult to test.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.100
It's great that
we have security,

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.660
but we want to be
able to test locally.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:41.420
So by adding our HTTP auth
bearer token when curling,

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.060
we can go and fix this issue.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:52.390
And so here, we'll just provide
a header with our authorization

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:55.380
token from gcloud.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:59.010
And you can see, Heyo, Martin!

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.270
MARTIN OMANDER: It works.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:01.330
Nice.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:03.330
I'll use that, for sure.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:07.770
So but, Grant, so this
worked, but whose access level

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.810
was used now for this command?

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.810
GRANT TIMMERMAN: That's
a really great question.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:15.770
So this command uses
the same authorization

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:18.680
as the person who
logged into that machine

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:22.590
with gcloud auth login.

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:25.110
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, nice.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:25.800
All right.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:29.610
For number six, sometimes
when using Google Cloud,

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:33.150
I'm supposed to use a project
number rather than a project

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:34.260
ID.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:36.840
This is, for example, when
I'm using service accounts

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.700
or creating service accounts.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.260
Is there a way to get
that project number

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:44.120
if I have the project ID?

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.390
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, yeah.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:50.035
So well, with a little
bit of gcloud-fu,

00:08:50.035 --> 00:08:55.460
you can go and get one
value from the other.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.930
So with this little command--

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.180
I use this all the time--

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.340
you can go and get
your project number

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.050
if you have your project ID.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:06.570
So what it does
in the background

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:10.320
is it lists your projects
and gets your project ID

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.360
and then formats it
just for that number.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.110
So you might use this with
different credentials,

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:21.250
or especially like
with Cloud Storage.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:24.830
And likewise, let's say you
have that project number,

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.155
but you want to get your ID.

00:09:26.155 --> 00:09:31.460
And so you can do the reverse.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:34.310
And you can go
list your projects

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:37.100
and get that project ID.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:39.567
And there, you get
your project ID.

00:09:39.567 --> 00:09:40.650
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:44.490
Thanks for showing me these six
incredibly useful tips, Grant.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.290
I didn't know you can
do all of that stuff

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.450
from the command line.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:51.060
GRANT TIMMERMAN: Oh, you're
quite welcome, Martin.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:55.060
I keep discovering new features
and new tips to myself.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.960
I guess you learn
something new every day

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:57.960
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, yes.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.720
I've learned six
new things today.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.320
Thank you, everyone,
for watching.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.290
We hope you found
these tips useful.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:07.410
And please enter any
questions or comments below.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:08.370
See you next time.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

